gcshape 1  into:/GraphCodeShape/Presets  smooth:0.1  shade:40

# DEBRIS — L3 Parts, the loose pieces broken wood leaves behind. Ready-made meshes, not modifiers.
# `Docs/GC-SHAPE-PRESET-LIBRARY.md` §12.2; method in `Docs/GC-SHAPE-WOOD-DAMAGE.md` (v2).
#
#   Debris = shape('/GraphCodeShape/Presets/Debris', Face = $Timber, Broken = $RawWood)
#   Yard   = mesh() { add($Debris.Splinters, count=12, sticks=0.25, noise=7) }
#
# THE FRAME, EVERY ENTRY: the grain runs along +Y, the width along X, the thickness up Z, each
# piece centred on the origin. A pile or a smashed board lies on z = 0 around the origin.
#
# WOOD IS DIRECTION-BASED, AND EVERYTHING HERE FOLLOWS THE GRAIN (owner ruling 2026-11-18):
#   · a piece is a lead STRAND (a lofted bundle of fibre) plus riders, a horn, a peeled ribbon and
#     the FRAY at each broken end, each laid along the grain.
#   · a lead strand's long faces are split TWO WAYS (round 4): three coarse BUNDLES a face that stand
#     proud or sink (`bundle`, `recess`) and stop at their own station, and inside them SIX RIBBONS a
#     wide face and three LAYERS an edge, each stepped up or down by up to `rib` of the width, tilted
#     (`tilt`), sliding sideways (`slide`), and proud either up to or from its own station, so every
#     long face is a stepped ridge profile along the grain, not a slab. Each bundle, ribbon and layer
#     reaches its own distance at a broken end (`jag`), the thickness breaks in three shelves (`step`).
#   · the FRAY: 10-30 thin ribbons (8-15 : 1 : 0.2) sunk into each broken end and standing out of it
#     at their own depth, place, roll and length, some bent up, down or sideways (`fray`, `zone`).
#   · hair-fine fibre is the TEXTURE layer (`SM_Debris_ShardBaked`), not geometry.
#   · the UVs are projected AFTER the strand is shaped, in the strand's own frame and before any
#     piece turns it: one `uv(box)` at a physical `tile` (cm per tile), so the long faces and every
#     torn wall carry the grain along Y at their true size, the end faces get end grain. `aspect`
#     stretches the along-grain tile (4 = a direction-revealing checker); `uoff`/`voff` shift a
#     piece's tiles so a pile does not repeat one patch of grain. A pile or a smashed board adds
#     pieces with `join=none` and writes `uv(keep)`, so every turned copy keeps its own grain.
#
# NUMBERS (`Docs/Research/wood-damage-references.md` rule 8, `wood-break-real-photos.md` 2-4, 9, 10):
# a shard is 3:1 to 8:1, thickness about half its width, one end tapered, the other ragged and
# blunt (`tip=` near 1 makes a chunk ragged at both ends); a splinter is the rarer thin ribbon,
# 8-15 : 1 : 0.35, flat-curved; chips are thin curved flakes 2:1-4:1, about a tenth of their length
# thick, curled 10-30 degrees; broken pieces keep a 5-30 degree kink or bow.
#
# TODAY'S SPELLINGS (2026-11-18): the installed editor refuses `noise()` in a warp, `output.outward`
# in a fracture body and a `random()` inside a `( … )` list, so the fields here are sine hashes,
# every draw is a `$Name` first, and `Smashed` places its pieces from `output.index` itself. Shape
# defects worked around, with the coders: an `if` inside a `loop` is not decided per pass (G17, so
# `Smashed` runs two loops); `random()` draws are keyed on the absolute file line (G16, so an edit
# above re-rolls the pile); a `[maps=]` bake replaces UV0 (G15, so the baked shard is its own line);
# box UV merges single-triangle islands (G18, so a torn end still stretches its bridging triangles).
# The sections NAME their points: a `points()` outline mixing short and long edges is resampled by
# the loft, and named keys keep it exact.
#
# GENERATED (round 4, temporary): the `Strand` warp is written by
# `Content/__MCPTests/WoodDamage/DebrisGen/` from named parts (listed as comments above it) because
# one formula cannot yet name its own sub-expressions; edit the generator, not the warp line.
#
# APPENDED, NOT WELDED. The strands of one piece overlap and are appended (`join=none`); a union
# of strands that overlap along their whole length leaves folds.
#
# NAMES AVOID THE GROUP-5 RESERVED SET: the seed is `noise=`, the tile size `tile=` (cm per tile).

build {
  SM_Debris_Shard      = $Ex_Shard
  SM_Debris_ShardBaked = $Ex_Shard      [maps=(normal, ao), from=$Ex_ShardDetail, resolution=1024]
  SM_Debris_ShardB     = $Ex_ShardB
  SM_Debris_ShardC     = $Ex_ShardC
  SM_Debris_Splinter   = $Ex_Splinter
  SM_Debris_SplinterB  = $Ex_SplinterB
  SM_Debris_Chips      = $Ex_Chips
  SM_Debris_ChipsB     = $Ex_ChipsB
  SM_Debris_Pile       = $Ex_Pile
  SM_Debris_PileB      = $Ex_PileB
  SM_Debris_Smashed    = $Ex_Smashed
  SM_Debris_SmashedB   = $Ex_SmashedB
}

Face   = material()
Broken = material()

# ---- the strand: one bundle of fibre --------------------------------------------------------------
StrandSection = plane() { points((-50, -38), (-12, -50), (50, -42), (50, 35), (8, 50), (-50, 45), (-50, 0), loop=true) }
StrandMid     = plane() { points((-46, -50), (-15, -50), (15, -50), (46, -50), (50, 0), (46, 50), (15, 50), (-15, 50), (-46, 50), (-50, 0), loop=true) }
# six ribbons a wide face, three layers an edge: each wall between two is a pair of points 1.4 apart,
# so a step is crisp (steeper than `shade:`); two more points inside each ribbon carry the ridges
# (round 5), 56 points in all
# a fray ribbon: a flat, uneven five-sided section
StrandRibbon  = plane() { points((-50, -50), (45, -50), (50, 15), (25, 50), (-50, 35), loop=true) }
StrandRib     = plane() { points(T0: (-50, -50), Ta: (-44.6, -50), Tb: (-39.0, -50), T1: (-34.0, -50), T2: (-32.6, -50), Tc: (-27.5, -50), Td: (-22.2, -50), T3: (-17.4, -50), T4: (-16.0, -50), Te: (-11.1, -50), Tf: (-5.6, -50), T5: (-0.7, -50), T6: (0.7, -50), Tg: (5.9, -50), Th: (10.8, -50), T7: (16.0, -50), T8: (17.4, -50), Ti: (22.6, -50), Tj: (27.9, -50), T9: (32.6, -50), T10: (34.0, -50), Tk: (39.4, -50), Tl: (44.9, -50), T11: (50, -50), R1: (50, -17.4), R2: (50, -16.0), R3: (50, 16.0), R4: (50, 17.4), B11: (50, 50), Bl: (44.3, 50), Bk: (38.8, 50), B10: (34.0, 50), B9: (32.6, 50), Bj: (27.2, 50), Bi: (21.9, 50), B8: (17.4, 50), B7: (16.0, 50), Bh: (11.4, 50), Bg: (5.2, 50), B6: (0.7, 50), B5: (-0.7, 50), Bf: (-6.1, 50), Be: (-10.6, 50), B4: (-16.0, 50), B3: (-17.4, 50), Bd: (-22.9, 50), Bc: (-28.1, 50), B2: (-32.6, 50), B1: (-34.0, 50), Bb: (-38.6, 50), Ba: (-45.1, 50), B0: (-50, 50), L4: (-50, 17.4), L3: (-50, 16.0), L2: (-50, -16.0), L1: (-50, -17.4), loop=true) }
# a flake: the six ribbons on both faces and no edge points (a chip's edge is its rim)
StrandFlake   = plane() { points(T0: (-50, -50), T1: (-34.0, -50), T2: (-32.6, -50), T3: (-17.4, -50), T4: (-16.0, -50), T5: (-0.7, -50), T6: (0.7, -50), T7: (16.0, -50), T8: (17.4, -50), T9: (32.6, -50), T10: (34.0, -50), T11: (50, -50), B11: (50, 50), B10: (34.0, 50), B9: (32.6, 50), B8: (17.4, 50), B7: (16.0, 50), B6: (0.7, 50), B5: (-0.7, 50), B4: (-16.0, 50), B3: (-17.4, 50), B2: (-32.6, 50), B1: (-34.0, 50), B0: (-50, 50), loop=true) }

# From `y0` for `length` along +Y, centred on x = z = 0. SIZE AND TAPER: `w0`/`t0` are the width
# and thickness left at the y0 end (reached over `a0` of the length), `w1`/`t1` at the far end
# (over `a1`); `chisel` moves a narrowed end off-centre (-1..1). ENDS: `lean`/`skew` slant each end
# face across the thickness and the width, `jag` tears it bundle by bundle and ribbon by ribbon (cm),
# `step` breaks the thickness in three shelves (a fraction of `thick` between the outer two); every
# end term pushes outward, never into the strand. BUNDLES (fractions of `thick`): `bundle` the most
# a bundle stands proud, `recess` how far below the face the lowest sinks (a fraction of `bundle`),
# `bundles` 1-3 across a face (1 = none). RIBBONS (fractions of `width`): `rib` the most a ribbon or
# edge layer steps up or down, `tilt` how much its top leans across it, `slide` how far the walls
# between ribbons slide (0-1). `st1`<`st2`<`st3` are the stations (of 100 along the strand) where a
# bundle or ribbon may stop or start, each 2 long. `edge` wobbles the edges ring by ring, `jit`
# roughens every point along its own face's normal, `wander` bows the whole strand; `flat` (0..1)
# keeps the top face planed (a board's own face). BEND: the y0 end rises `lift` and swings `sway`
# (fractions of `length`) over the first `reach` of the strand (of 100). `fine` picks the section:
# 1 the ribboned 56-point one on nine rings, 2 the 24-point flake on six, 0 the 10-point and -1
# the 7-point on three, -2 the 5-point fray ribbon on four. RIDGES: `ridge` (fraction of `width`) the
# height of the ridge points; `swell` thickens and thins the strand along its length (fraction). SURFACE: `tile` cm per UV tile, `aspect` the along-grain tile over the
# across one, `uoff`/`voff` a shift in tiles.
Strand = mesh(length=12.0, width=1.0, thick=0.8, y0=-6.0, w0=0.9, t0=0.9, a0=0.2, w1=0.3, t1=0.6, a1=0.5, lean0=0.0, skew0=0.0, jag0=0.1, step0=0.0, lean1=0.0, skew1=0.0, jag1=0.1, step1=0.0, chisel=0.0, bundle=0.1, bundles=3, recess=0.4, rib=0.0, tilt=0.5, slide=1.0, edge=0.0, st1=18.0, st2=46.0, st3=76.0, jit=0.03, wander=0.04, flat=0, lift=0.0, sway=0.0, reach=30.0, ridge=0.0, swell=0.0, fine=0, tile=100.0, aspect=1.0, uoff=0.0, voff=0.0, noise=1) {
  if ($fine > 1.5) {
    add($StrandFlake)
    loft(through=($StrandFlake, $StrandFlake, $StrandFlake, $StrandFlake, $StrandFlake, $StrandFlake), heights=($st1 * 0.5, $st1, $st1 + 2, $st2, $st2 + 2, 100))
  } else {
    if ($fine > 0.5) {
      add($StrandRib)
      loft(through=($StrandRib, $StrandRib, $StrandRib, $StrandRib, $StrandRib, $StrandRib, $StrandRib, $StrandRib), heights=($st1 * 0.5, $st1, $st1 + 2, $st2, $st2 + 2, $st3, $st3 + 2, 100))
    } else {
      if ($fine < -1.5) {
        add($StrandRibbon)
        loft(through=($StrandRibbon, $StrandRibbon, $StrandRibbon), heights=(33, 66, 100))
      } else {
        if ($fine < -0.5) {
          add($StrandSection)
          loft(through=($StrandSection, $StrandSection), heights=(50, 100))
        } else {
          add($StrandMid)
          loft(through=($StrandMid, $StrandMid), heights=(50, 100))
        }
      }
    }
  }
  # the warp below is GENERATED from these named parts (DebrisGen/strand.py):
  #   TW     = (1 - (1 - $w0) * clamp(1 - point.z / 100 / $a0, 0, 1) - (1 - $w1) * clamp((point.z / 100 - 1 + $a1) / $a1, 0, 1))
  #   TT     = (1 - (1 - $t0) * clamp(1 - point.z / 100 / $a0, 0, 1) - (1 - $t1) * clamp((point.z / 100 - 1 + $a1) / $a1, 0, 1))
  #   TOPM   = clamp((-point.y - 40) / 5, 0, 1)
  #   BOTM   = clamp((point.y - 40) / 5, 0, 1)
  #   SIDE   = (clamp((point.x - 46) / 3, 0, 1) - clamp((-point.x - 46) / 3, 0, 1))
  #   FACE   = (TOPM + BOTM)
  #   F      = clamp(floor(point.y / 50 + 1), 0, 1)
  #   CU     = min(5, max(0, floor((point.x + 50) / 16.67)))
  #   RID    = (CU + 6 * F)
  #   BID    = (floor(CU * clamp($bundles, 1, 3) / 6) + 3 * F)
  #   LY     = min(2, max(0, floor((point.y + 50) / 33.34)))
  #   SS     = clamp(floor(point.x / 50 + 1), 0, 1)
  #   SID    = (LY + 3 * SS + 12)
  #   CID    = (FACE * BID + (1 - FACE) * SID)
  #   EID    = (FACE * RID + (1 - FACE) * SID)
  #   LYA    = (BOTM * 2 + (1 - FACE) * LY)
  #   POSR   = (FACE * ((point.x + 50 - 16.67 * CU) / 8.33 - 1))
  #   PB     = floor(2 + 1.99 * sin(CID * 3511.7 + $noise * 47.9))
  #   PR     = floor(2 + 1.99 * sin(EID * 1931.9 + $noise * 29.7))
  #   ZEB    = ($st1 + 2 + clamp(PB, 0, 1) * ($st2 - $st1) + clamp(PB - 1, 0, 1) * ($st3 - $st2) + clamp(PB - 2, 0, 1) * (106 - $st3))
  #   ZER    = ($st1 + 2 + clamp(PR, 0, 1) * ($st2 - $st1) + clamp(PR - 1, 0, 1) * ($st3 - $st2) + clamp(PR - 2, 0, 1) * (106 - $st3))
  #   COARSE = ($bundle * ((1 + $recess) * (0.5 + 0.5 * sin(CID * 7823.1 + $noise * 91.3)) - $recess) * (0.6 + 0.25 * sin(point.z * 6.7 + CID * 977.3 + $noise * 23.9) + 0.15 * sin(point.z * 41.3 + CID * 311.9 + $noise * 7.1)) * clamp((ZEB - point.z) / 2, 0, 1))
  #   QR     = clamp(floor(1 + sin(EID * 2741.3 + $noise * 83.9)), 0, 1)
  #   CUTR   = (QR + (1 - 2 * QR) * clamp((ZER - point.z) / 2, 0, 1))
  #   FINE   = ($rib * (sin(EID * 5381.7 + $noise * 33.1) + $tilt * sin(EID * 4409.3 + $noise * 57.1) * POSR) * (0.55 + 0.2 * sin(point.z * 8.3 + EID * 613.1 + $noise * 41.3) + 0.25 * sin(point.z * 53.7 + EID * 271.3 + $noise * 13.9)) * (0.35 + 0.65 * CUTR))
  #   RIDGE  = ($ridge * (sin(point.x * 37.9 + F * 91.7 + $noise * 63.7) + 0.5 * sin(point.x * 11.3 - F * 27.1 + $noise * 19.3)) * (0.65 + 0.35 * sin(point.z * 29.3 + point.x * 17.1 + $noise * 5.3)))
  #   JN     = ($jit * sin(point.x * 13.11 - point.y * 5.77 + $noise * 177.3) * (0.6 + 0.4 * sin(point.z * 5.4 + 180 * sin(point.x * 7.919 + point.y * 13.37 + $noise * 311.7))))
  #   RELA   = (clamp(point.z, 0, 1) * clamp(100 - point.z, 0, 1) * ($thick * TT * COARSE + min($width * TW, 1.5 * $thick * TT) * (FINE + RIDGE)) + $thick * TT * JN)
  #   KF     = (floor((point.x + 58.33) / 16.67) + 7 * F)
  #   KS     = (floor((point.y + 66.67) / 33.34) + 4 * SS)
  #   WALLM  = clamp(2 - abs(point.x - 16.67 * floor(point.x / 16.67 + 0.5)), 0, 1)
  #   SHX    = (clamp((45 - abs(point.x)) / 5, 0, 1) * WALLM * $slide * (2.5 * sin(KF * 1291.3 + $noise * 73.1) + 1.0 * sin(point.z * 4.1 + KF * 977.7 + $noise * 29.3)))
  #   SHY    = (clamp((45 - abs(point.y)) / 5, 0, 1) * abs(SIDE) * $slide * (6 * sin(KS * 2203.9 + $noise * 61.7) + 2 * sin(point.z * 3.7 + KS * 811.3 + $noise * 19.9)))
  #   WOB    = ($edge * sin(point.z * 9.1 + F * 77.7 + SIDE * 131.3 + $noise * 43.1))
  #   FLAT   = (1 - $flat * TOPM)
  #   BEND   = (clamp(1 - point.z / $reach, 0, 1) * clamp(1 - point.z / $reach, 0, 1))
  #   E0     = clamp(1 - point.z, 0, 1)
  #   E1     = clamp(point.z - 99, 0, 1)
  #   JAG0   = ($jag0 * (0.55 * sin(CID * 1733.3 + $noise * 37.7) + 0.35 * sin(EID * 2311.9 + $noise * 71.3) + 0.1 * sin(point.x * 13.11 - point.y * 5.77 + $noise * 177.3) - 1))
  #   JAG1   = ($jag1 * (0.55 * sin(CID * 2917.9 + $noise * 53.3) + 0.35 * sin(EID * 1409.7 + $noise * 23.3) + 0.1 * sin(point.x * 7.919 + point.y * 13.37 + $noise * 311.7) + 1))
  #   STEP0  = ($step0 * $thick * (0.5 - LYA / 2 - 0.5 * clamp($step0 * 1000, -1, 1)))
  #   STEP1  = ($step1 * $thick * (LYA / 2 - 0.5 + 0.5 * clamp($step1 * 1000, -1, 1)))
  #   LEAN0  = ($lean0 * $thick * (-point.y / 100 - 0.5 * clamp($lean0 * 1000, -1, 1)) + $skew0 * $width * (point.x / 100 - 0.5 * clamp($skew0 * 1000, -1, 1)))
  #   LEAN1  = ($lean1 * $thick * (-point.y / 100 + 0.5 * clamp($lean1 * 1000, -1, 1)) + $skew1 * $width * (point.x / 100 + 0.5 * clamp($skew1 * 1000, -1, 1)))
  #   X      = $width * (TW * (point.x + SHX) / 100 + $chisel * 0.5 * (1 - TW) + $wander * sin(point.z * 2.7 + $noise * 97.1)) + SIDE * (RELA + $thick * TT * WOB) + $sway * $length * BEND
  #   Y      = $y0 + $length * point.z / 100 + E0 * (LEAN0 + JAG0 + STEP0) + E1 * (LEAN1 + JAG1 + STEP1)
